{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_a56400d0f8274b55bb7ed56b","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The planning_retry_test.go uses `command.NewPlanningRetryGate(fakeGit, taskDir, clock, fakeCommenter, metrics.New())`. The gate's modify functions need vault name too. I need to thread vaultName into `NewPlanningRetryGate` as well. Let me check the planning_retry.go more carefully — it calls `buildRetryModifyFn` and `buildEscalationModifyFn`. These need the vault name for healing.\n\nThe prompt says:\n> Apply it in the result write path (buildResultModifyFn), in the update-frontmatter and increment-frontmatter modify functions, in the complete executor's write (buildCompleteModifyFn), and in BOTH planning-retry-gate write paths (buildRetryModifyFn and buildEscalationModifyFn in pkg/command/planning_retry.go — the gate runs after the routing predicate in NewTaskResultExecutor and returns handled=true, so the executor returns before WriteResult and the result-path heal never fires for these writes).\n\nSo I need to thread vaultName into the planning retry gate as well. Let me check how the gate is constructed — in factory.go: `command.NewPlanningRetryGate(gitClient, taskDir, currentDateTime, prCommenter, m)`. And in planning_retry_test.go: `command.NewPlanningRetryGate(fakeGit, taskDir, clock, fakeCommenter, metrics.New())`.\n\nSo I need to add vaultName param to NewPlanningRetryGate too.\n\nNow let me think about the design.\n\n## Design\n\n### 1. Routing predicate (pkg/routing)\n\nAdd `ShouldProcessFrontmatterCommand(targetVault, vaultName string) bool`:\n```go\n// ShouldProcessFrontmatterCommand returns true iff the controller's vaultName owns\n// this frontmatter command (update-frontmatter, increment-frontmatter, complete).\n// An empty targetVault falls through to true — legacy unstamped commands are\n// processed by every controller; the write path's heal-on-write stamps target_vault\n// so the non-owning controller permanently stops falling through. A non-empty\n// targetVault is compared verbatim to vaultName.\nfunc ShouldProcessFrontmatterCommand(targetVault, vaultName string) bool {\n\tif targetVault == \"\" {\n\t\treturn true\n\t}\n\treturn targetVault == vaultName\n}\n```\n\nWait — but there's a subtlety. The prompt says:\n> The empty-targetVault fall-through is load-bearing for legacy unstamped tasks: both controllers attempt, the owner finds and heals the file, the non-owner drops — do NOT \"fix\" it by defaulting to openclaw (that would route legacy personal-vault tasks to the wrong controller permanently)\n\nSo empty targetVault → true (both controllers process). This matches ShouldProcessResult semantics.\n\n### 2. Thread VaultName into executors\n\n- `NewUpdateFrontmatterExecutor(gitClient, taskDir, vaultName, m)` \n- `NewIncrementFrontmatterExecutor(gitClient, taskDir, vaultName, m)`\n- `NewCompleteTaskExecutor(gitClient, taskDir, currentDateTime, vaultName, m)`\n- `NewPlanningRetryGate(gitClient, taskDir, vaultName, currentDateTime, prCommenter, m)` — wait, order. Let me pick a consistent order: vaultName after taskDir maybe. Let me check the existing NewTaskResultExecutor(writer, retryGate, vaultName) — vaultName is last there. And NewCreateTaskExecutor(gitClient, taskDir, vaultName, currentDateTime, k) — vaultName third.\n\nLet me check NewCreateTaskExecutor's signature.\n\nActually, let me keep it simple and put vaultName right after taskDir for the three frontmatter executors and the gate. Let me check the create executor signature to be consistent.\n\nActually, let me look at how NewCreateTaskExecutor is defined to pick the ordering convention.\n\nLet me grep for it.\n\nIn factory.go:\n```go\ncommand.NewCreateTaskExecutor(gitClient, taskDir, vaultName, currentDateTime, k),\n```\n\nSo the convention is vaultName after taskDir. I'll follow:\n- `NewUpdateFrontmatterExecutor(gitClient, taskDir, vaultName, m)`\n- `NewIncrementFrontmatterExecutor(gitClient, taskDir, vaultName, m)`\n- `NewCompleteTaskExecutor(gitClient, taskDir, vaultName, currentDateTime, m)`\n- `NewPlanningRetryGate(gitClient, taskDir, vaultName, currentDateTime, prCommenter, m)`\n\nHmm, but for the result writer, NewResultWriter is constructed in main.go. I need to add vaultName there too:\n- `result.NewResultWriter(gitClient, taskDir, vaultName, currentDateTime, m, waiter)` — or keep it with the other params. The prompt says \"both need the vault name for this\".\n\nLet me decide ordering for NewResultWriter: `NewResultWriter(gitClient, taskDir, vaultName, currentDateTime, m, waiter)`. Since the result writer is in main.go and test files. Actually, maybe put vaultName last to minimize confusion? Let me look at the signature — actually it's a constructor in our own code, so I can pick any order. Let me follow the create-executor convention of putting vaultName after taskDir: `NewResultWriter(gitClient, taskDir, vaultName, currentDateTime, m, waiter)`.\n\n### 3. Guard in executors\n\nIn each executor, after deserializing the command (and validating), before the task-file lookup:\n\n```go\nif !routing.ShouldProcessFrontmatterCommand(cmd.TargetVault, vaultName) {\n    glog.V(2).Infof(\n        \"update-frontmatter: skipped vault mismatch target_vault=%q vault=%q task=%s\",\n        cmd.TargetVault, vaultName, cmd.TaskIdentifier,\n    )\n    return nil, nil, errors.Wrapf(\n        ctx,\n        cdb.ErrCommandObjectSkipped,\n        \"cross-vault update-frontmatter for task %s\",\n        cmd.TaskIdentifier,\n    )\n}\n```\n\nImportant subtlety: for update-frontmatter, the existing \"empty updates\" no-op check happens BEFORE. Should the routing guard come before or after the empty-updates check? The task says \"apply the predicate before the task-file lookup in each executor\". The empty updates no-op is a shortcut before lookup. I think routing guard should come before the no-op check too — a cross-vault command should be skipped with ErrCommandObjectSkipped even if it's a no-op. Actually, wait — think carefully. If an empty-updates command for another vault comes through, returning nil would publish a spurious Success event on the shared result topic. So the routing guard should come FIRST, before the empty-updates no-op check. Let me place it right after deserialize and before the no-op check.\n\nActually for update-frontmatter: the current code deserializes then checks empty updates. I'll insert the routing guard right after deserialize, before the empty-updates no-op.\n\nFor increment-frontmatter: after deserialize, before FindTaskFilePath.\n\nFor complete-task: after deserialize and validate TaskIdentifier, before FindTaskFilePath. Actually let me place it right after deserialize, before Validate. Order: deserialize → routing → validate → find. Hmm, the task says \"apply the predicate before the task-file lookup in each executor\". Placing before validate is fine too. But to be consistent with task_result_executor.go, where routing comes after validation... Let me check: in NewTaskResultExecutor, the order is: deserialize → Validate → ShouldProcessResult → retryGate → WriteResult. So validation happens before routing there.\n\nFor consistency, I'll place routing after validation but before the task-file lookup:\n- update: deserialize → (empty-updates no-op? No...) \n\nHmm wait. For update-frontmatter, the empty-updates no-op check returns nil BEFORE validation. Where to put the routing guard? Let me think about what's cleanest and matches the intent.\n\nThe intent: cross-vault commands must be skipped with ErrCommandObjectSkipped (not nil). So the guard must be before any nil-return path that would otherwise publish a spurious Success event. The empty-updates path returns nil → publishes spurious Success. So the routing guard must come before the empty-updates check.\n\nSo for update-frontmatter: deserialize → routing guard → empty-updates no-op → find task file...\n\nFor increment-frontmatter: deserialize → routing guard → find task file.\n\nFor complete: deserialize → routing guard → validate TaskIdentifier → find task file. (Or validate then routing — doesn't matter much, but for consistency with result executor where validate is before routing, and since complete does validate cmd.TaskIdentifier... Let me place routing after deserialize but before validate — actually I'll follow the result executor: validate first, then routing. Hmm but for complete, the current code validates TaskIdentifier. I'll keep validate first then routing, then find.)\n\nWait, actually let me reconsider. In the result executor, validation of the incoming Task happens first, then routing. The rationale there is that a malformed command is always an error regardless of vault. But for frontmatter commands, the commands are simple. Let me just be pragmatic:\n\n- update: deserialize → routing guard → empty-updates no-op → find\n- increment: deserialize → routing guard → find\n- complete: deserialize → routing guard → validate → find\n\nThis ensures the guard is always the first skip decision and no cross-vault command ever returns nil.\n\n### 4. Heal-on-write\n\nAdd a helper in pkg/result beside MergeFrontmatter:\n\n```go\n// HealTargetVault stamps the controller's vaultName into frontmatter when the\n// target_vault key is absent. An existing value (any value) is never overridden.\n// Legacy task files created before target_vault stamping permanently stop\n// double-scanning once the owning controller writes them.\nfunc HealTargetVault(fm lib.TaskFrontmatter, vaultName string) {\n\tif _, ok := fm[\"target_vault\"]; !ok {\n\t\tfm[\"target_vault\"] = vaultName\n\t}\n}\n```\n\nThen apply it:\n- In `buildResultModifyFn` (result_writer.go): after MergeFrontmatter, on `merged`. But wait — the result path. The result's own frontmatter may carry `target_vault` (echoed by agent). MergeFrontmatter merges existing + incoming. If incoming carries target_vault, merged has it. If neither has it, we heal. So call `HealTargetVault(merged, r.vaultName)` after MergeFrontmatter.\n\nActually, should the result writer heal on the merged map? Yes — the requirement: \"when the write path's merged frontmatter has no target_vault key, set it to the controller's vault name in the same write; an existing value (any value) is never overridden.\"\n\nSo the heal should run on the merged frontmatter that will be written.\n\n- In `buildUpdateModifyFn` (task_update_frontmatter_executor.go): the executor builds `fm` from the on-disk frontmatter and applies updates. Add `result.HealTargetVault(fm, vaultName)` before marshal. Need to thread vaultName into buildUpdateModifyFn.\n\n- In `buildIncrementModifyFn` (task_increment_frontmatter_executor.go): same, add heal. Need vaultName threaded in.\n\n- In `buildCompleteModifyFn` (task_complete_task_executor.go): same, add heal. Need vaultName threaded in.\n\n- In `buildRetryModifyFn` and `buildEscalationModifyFn` (planning_retry.go): same, add heal. The gate needs vaultName.\n\n- NOT in `buildSupersedeModifyFn` (task_create_task_executor.go) — prior files written to terminal status; create commands route through ShouldProcess.\n\nNow, where exactly to call heal in each modify fn:\n\nFor buildUpdateModifyFn: after `result.ClearAssigneeIfHumanReview(fm)` and before `return marshalFileContent(ctx, fm, body)`. Actually order doesn't matter much; heal is additive. But to be safe, heal should be after the update merge (so an existing target_vault in updates is never overridden) — heal only sets when absent. Let me place it right before marshal.\n\nFor buildCompleteModifyFn: before `return marshalFileContent(ctx, fm, closedBody)`. Note the idempotency early-return `return nil, nil` — that's a skip, no write, no heal needed (well, actually if we skip writing we don't heal — fine, that's the \"not already resolved\" guard; the requirement only says heal \"when the write path's merged frontmatter has no target_vault key\" — a no-op write isn't a write path).\n\nFor