{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_c12ca6861f2c49ab9200f454","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"go mod tidy works. Now let me check the coverage of the new code paths specifically. Let me run coverage on the affected packages.\n\nActually, before that, let me verify the changed packages have ≥80% coverage with the -coverprofile. But first, let me update the docs and CHANGELOG (task 6). Then I'll do the final coverage check and precommit.\n\nLet me update docs/controller-design.md:\n1. Command Processing paragraph (line 50) — extend to describe frontmatter-command routing + heal rule, empty-targetVault fall-through semantics.\n2. Atomic Frontmatter Commands section (lines 132-180) — add routing guard step + heal step; note the complete executor there too.\n\nLet me write the doc updates.\n\nFor the Command Processing paragraph (line 50), I'll extend the existing paragraph about ShouldProcess with a new paragraph about frontmatter commands + heal.\n\nLet me draft:\n\n```\nThe controller reads a required `VAULT_NAME` env var (CLI flag `--vault-name`) at startup naming the single Obsidian vault it serves. Every CreateCommand is checked against `VAULT_NAME` via the `pkg/routing.ShouldProcess` predicate: the effective target is `cmd.targetVault` if non-empty, otherwise the legacy fallback `openclaw`; commands whose effective target is not `VAULT_NAME` are skipped without side effects (no git write, no result publish, no error) and emit a single `glog.V(2)` line naming the command's `targetVault`, the effective target, and `VAULT_NAME` so operators can confirm routing decisions. Two controllers (e.g. one per vault) can therefore share the `agent-task-v1-request` topic without duplicating task materializations. The `targetVault` field is added to `task.CreateCommand` with `omitempty`; legacy producers that emit no `targetVault` continue to flow to the `openclaw` controller.\n\nThe frontmatter commands (`update-frontmatter`, `increment-frontmatter`, `complete-task`) carry the same `targetVault` (lib v0.86.0) and are guarded by `pkg/routing.ShouldProcessFrontmatterCommand` with the RESULT semantics, not the create semantics: an empty `targetVault` falls through to `true` — every controller attempts the command, the owning vault finds the file and heals it, the non-owning vault drops it. The routing guard runs BEFORE the task-file lookup, so a cross-vault command performs no vault scan, increments no metric, and publishes no result event (it returns an error wrapping `cdb.ErrCommandObjectSkipped`; a nil return would publish a spurious Success event on the shared result topic). A non-empty `targetVault` mismatch is skipped the same way.\n\nThe heal is the other half of the routing rule: every write path that touches a task file (result write-back, update-frontmatter, increment-frontmatter, complete-task, and both planning-retry-gate writes) stamps `target_vault` onto the file when the key is absent, recording the writing controller's `VAULT_NAME` in the same write. An existing `target_vault` (any value) is never overridden. A legacy unstamped file therefore falls through to both controllers exactly once: the owner writes and stamps it, after which `ShouldProcessResult`/`ShouldProcessFrontmatterCommand` return false for the non-owner forever. The supersede prior-file write (terminal `aborted`) intentionally does not heal — prior files are terminal and create commands already route through `ShouldProcess` into the owning vault.\n```\n\nFor the Atomic Frontmatter Commands section, I'll update the intro and add routing-guard + heal steps to the two subsections, plus add the complete executor. Let me update:\n\n```\n## Atomic Frontmatter Commands\n\nIn addition to the `\"update\"` operation (full result write), the controller handles three atomic frontmatter operations on `agent-task-v1-request`, each guarded by `pkg/routing.ShouldProcessFrontmatterCommand` and each healing `target_vault` on write:\n\n### `\"increment-frontmatter\"` (IncrementFrontmatterExecutor)\n\nPayload: `lib.IncrementFrontmatterCommand{TaskIdentifier, Field, Delta, TargetVault}`\n\n```\nOn agent-task-v1-request (operation: \"increment-frontmatter\"):\n  │\n  ├── deserialize IncrementFrontmatterCommand\n  ├── routing guard: non-empty TargetVault != VAULT_NAME → glog.V(2) skip line, no counter, no write, return ErrCommandObjectSkipped\n  ├── find task file by task_identifier (WalkDir)\n  ...\n  │     ├── set Field = newVal\n  │     ├── heal-on-write: if target_vault absent → stamp VAULT_NAME (never overrides an existing value)\n  ...\n```\n\nSimilarly for update-frontmatter and complete-task.\n\nFor complete-task, add a subsection:\n\n### `\"complete-task\"` (CompleteTaskExecutor)\n\nPayload: `lib.CompleteCommand{TaskIdentifier, RecoverySHA, TargetVault}`\n\n```\nOn agent-task-v1-request (operation: \"complete-task\"):\n  │\n  ├── deserialize CompleteCommand\n  ├── routing guard: non-empty TargetVault != VAULT_NAME → glog.V(2) skip line, no counter, no write, return ErrCommandObjectSkipped\n  ├── find task file by task_identifier (WalkDir)\n  ├── if not found → log warning, return nil\n  ├── AtomicReadModifyWriteAndCommitPush:\n  │     ├── read current file bytes (under mutex)\n  │     ├── if a # Resolution / ## Resolution section is already present → return nil, nil (idempotent no-op)\n  │     ├── set status: completed, phase: done, completed_date/processed_at: now, recovery_sha (when provided)\n  │     ├── heal-on-write: if target_vault absent → stamp VAULT_NAME (never overrides an existing value)\n  │     ├── append ## Resolution body section\n  │     ├── write updated file (under mutex)\n  │     └── git commit + push (under mutex)\n  └── increment FrontmatterCommandsTotal{operation, outcome}\n```\n\nNow let me make these edits.","signature":""}],"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4d516ec6-9c37-415d-ba1d-85d6d6e1eaa0","uuid":"e8e98a75-6c66-4a3d-ae53-4c10de6554cc","timestamp":"2026-09-03T17:51:06.997Z"}
